#[doc("""
Turning an interrupt into a cancellation, so an interrupted run cleans up
after itself.

A kalku leads its own process group, which is what lets a kill reach the
runtime a launcher started. The cost is that an interrupt no longer
reaches a kalku: Ctrl-C goes to the terminal's foreground group, and a
kalku is not in it. Left alone, that would make the interrupt kill the one
process that knows how to kill the others, and every kalku would be left
holding the reni's build lock — the hang this whole mechanism exists to
prevent.

So the signal is trapped and sent on as `Cancel`, the message a run
already understands: dispatching stops, what was measured is kept, and the
scope unwinds through the ward, which kills each group on the way out. An
interrupted run leaves nothing behind, which is the same promise as a run
that ends.

`SigTerm` as well as `SigInt`, because a supervisor stopping a server
sends the first and a person at a terminal the second, and both have to
leave the project as they found it.
""")]

import kalku.orchestrator.kalku_proc

#[doc("""
Run `body` with the signals that mean *stop* turned into `Cancel` for the
fiber that called.

The traps are set here and released here, around the run they belong to.
Left to the fiber that waits, they outlived the run: that fiber is still
waiting when the run has ended, and a signal sent then was caught, turned
into a message for a mailbox nobody reads, and lost. A server that had
finished one run could no longer be stopped.

One signal is enough: a second one arriving while the run unwinds would
be asking the cleanup to abandon itself, so it is caught and not heard.
""")]
pub fn interruptible[a, e](
  body: () -> a / Actor[OrchMsg] + e
) : a / Signal + Spawn + Actor[OrchMsg] + e {
  let run = Actor.self()
  trap()
  let _waiting = spawn_actor(() => told_to_stop(run, Cancel))
  let out = body()
  release()
  out
}

#[doc("Catch the signals that mean *stop*, for a caller that waits with `told_to_stop`.")]
pub fn trap() : Unit / Signal {
  Signal.on(SigInt)
  Signal.on(SigTerm)
}

#[doc("Give the signals that mean *stop* back to what they do by default.")]
pub fn release() : Unit / Signal {
  Signal.off(SigInt)
  Signal.off(SigTerm)
}

#[doc("Blocks until a trapped signal arrives, and then sends `what` to `whom`.")]
pub fn told_to_stop(whom: Pid[OrchMsg], what: OrchMsg) : Unit / Signal + Actor[OrchMsg] {
  let _which = Signal.await()
  Actor.send(whom, what)
}
